*** Defaults that differ between the products: 67 of 171

* Done -- 2026-11-16 (clautolisp 2.4.21)

All six points, per the dialect's product (a dialect without one --
clautolisp, strict, lax -- follows AutoCAD), in cador's HOST-SETVAR
(VALIDATE-SETVAR-VALUE, sysvar-api.lisp):

1. Out-of-range integers are refused -- AutoCAD ERRNO 22, BricsCAD 0 --
   against *SYSVAR-INTEGER-RANGES*: 482 ranges from the inventory plus the
   measured CMDECHO (0 1), widened per product by the 15 B5 overrides
   (generated sysvar-validation-data.lisp; scripts/generate-sysvar-
   validation-data.py, re-run after the inventory or a capture changes).
   Outside 16 bits: AutoCAD refuses (22), BricsCAD wraps to 16 bits first
   (OSMODE 70000 -> 4464, USERI1 40000 -> -25536), for :SHORT variables and
   :INTEGER ones whose range fits in 16 bits; an :INTEGER without a range
   takes any integer, as before (HPMAXLINES is 32-bit).
2. ERRNO: read-only 20 and unknown 0 on both; a bad type 87 (AutoCAD) / 0
   (BricsCAD); a non-string into a string variable 0 on both. The runtime
   error carries the product's ERRNO (AUTOLISP-RUNTIME-ERROR's new :ERRNO,
   SIGNAL-AUTOLISP-RUNTIME-ERROR-ERRNO).
3. SETVAR returns the value GIVEN.
4. A symbol name is accepted: (setvar 'OSMODE 1).
5. CMDECHO 0.7: refused by AutoCAD (87), truncated to 0 by BricsCAD.
6. SNAPBASE '(1 2 3) (a 2D variable): refused by AutoCAD (ERRNO 0), its 2D
   part kept by BricsCAD.

Tests: cador/tests/setvar-validation-tests.lisp, each case the measured
(RESULT STORED ERRNO); the whole clautolisp suite stays green.

Caveat: B5 measured 92 of the 482 ranges; the others are the vendors'
documentation. A documented range narrower than the product's would now
refuse a value the product takes: a later capture widens it through the
generator.

